Release 0.1.0.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 22:35:35

Game Mode shortcuts are named with the system first, "[PSP] Title" (owner, 15-10-2026), so Steam's menus, such as the
recent games in the tray icon's menu, tell a game apart from the same title on Steam or on another system. A shortcut
in Steam with another name than the app gives it now, as one from before, turns Update Game Mode on; the update
renames it, keeps its play time, and removes the artwork under its old Steam number (core's write_shortcuts).

Changelog: 15-10-2026 21:03:51

images/bc250-ppsspp-manager-128.png: the 128 px version of the icon, scaled from the 512 px file, so the app's images
folder has both sizes like the other apps. It is the source for the portal's tile icon.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 19:18:30

Rename files (owner's decision 15-10-2026): the standalone Bazzite UMD File Renamer is absorbed into this app and
dropped from the suite. The button opens a window with the ISO and CSO images the app found and the name each would
get from its own PARAM.SFO: the standalone's eight naming templates, character cleaning, per-game translation (with
Google Translate in the browser), the pictures cycling through ICON0, PIC0 and PIC1, and Rename all, which never takes
a name another file has. The reading is this app's own (games.py), so a CSO renames too, which the standalone, like
the C++ tool it ported, did not read; a CHD is not read and a memory stick game is never renamed (its file must stay
EBOOT.PBP). Left out of the port, served by the app itself: Open Folder and Open ISO (the game folders feed the
window), the PPSSPP path setting and launch (Play uses the pinned PPSSPP), the firmware field and the metallic blue
theme. The naming template is remembered in the app's settings; the standalone's saved choice is adopted once.
install.sh removes an installed standalone (launcher, menu entry, Desktop icon, program files); its settings folder
stays. The window says that a renamed game in Game Mode needs Update Game Mode, and the app says so again after the
window closes.

Changelog: 15-10-2026 17:35:00

New app, PPSSPP Manager (0.1.0, decided 15-10-2026 by the owner): PSP games on the BC-250 with PPSSPP, built as the
RPCS3 Manager is. Install downloads the PPSSPP 1.20.4 Linux AppImage from its GitHub release and checks it against
the SHA-256 the app was tried with; Remove takes it out and keeps PPSSPP's own folder (~/.config/ppsspp: saves,
settings, memory stick games). The app finds the games in the chosen folders (ISO and CSO images read for their
PARAM.SFO, title, disc ID and pictures; a CHD listed under its file name; EBOOT.PBP folders) and on PPSSPP's memory
stick (PSP/GAME, a game's update with category PG left out), and puts the ticked ones in Steam for Game Mode with
artwork drawn from the game's own ICON0.PNG and PIC1.PNG. The Game Mode shortcuts run ~/.local/bin/bc250-ppsspp-run
(--fullscreen --pause-menu-exit), which reads a real controller itself while one is connected and opens the window
through X11 when Steam starts a game on a Wayland desktop, so Steam's overlay works. The app says when PPSSPP
published a newer release (its tags have two or three numbers, read by the app itself); it never installs one it was
not tried with. PPSSPP does not update itself on Linux. The window icon is a placeholder until the owner's images
are ready.
